import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/jmis01.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "What JMIS Is — IFRS With Two Deliberate Modifications" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Scope of This Modification" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Modification Itself — Mandatory Goodwill Amortisation" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Why the ASBJ Insisted on This Departure From IFRS" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Consistency With J-GAAP's Own Goodwill Treatment" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Impairment Testing Continues Alongside Amortisation" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Goodwill Under JMIS vs Unmodified IFRS" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — an Impairment Trigger Under JMIS" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hub">
      <div class="hub-box">
        <div class="hub-title">JMIS Modification Accounting Standard No. 1 — Accounting for Goodwill</div>
        <div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · Japan's Modified International Standards (JMIS) · Requires goodwill amortisation, departing from IFRS 3/IAS 36 · JPY examples</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="toc">
      <a href="#s1">1. Conceptual Framework Linkage</a><a href="#s2">2. What JMIS Is — IFRS With Two Deliberate Modifications</a><a href="#s3">3. Scope of This Modification</a>
      <a href="#s4">4. The Modification Itself — Mandatory Goodwill Amortisation</a><a href="#s5">5. Why the ASBJ Insisted on This Departure From IFRS</a>
      <a href="#s6">6. Consistency With J-GAAP's Own Goodwill Treatment</a><a href="#s7">7. Impairment Testing Continues Alongside Amortisation</a>
      <a href="#s8">8. Worked Example — Goodwill Under JMIS vs Unmodified IFRS</a><a href="#s9">9. Worked Example — an Impairment Trigger Under JMIS</a>
      <a href="#s10">10. Disclosure Requirements</a><a href="#s11">11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</a>
      <a href="#s12">12. Relationship to IAS/IFRS</a><a href="#s13">13. Statement Mapping</a><a href="#s14">14. Related Standards</a><a href="#s15">15. Glossary</a>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A deliberate, narrow carve-out preserving J-GAAP's own long-standing preference</div>
        <div class="principle">JMIS Modification Accounting Standard No. 1 amends how GOODWILL is subsequently accounted for WITHIN the JMIS framework — requiring SYSTEMATIC AMORTISATION over a determined period, RATHER than the "no amortisation, impairment-only" approach unmodified IFRS 3 and IAS 36 prescribe internationally. This is JMIS's SINGLE most significant, best-known modification, and it directly IMPORTS J-GAAP's OWN long-standing goodwill philosophy (see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 21 poster) into a framework OTHERWISE built on IFRS.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>What JMIS Is — IFRS With Two Deliberate Modifications</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Japan's own "endorsed IFRS," modified in exactly two respects</div>
        <div class="principle">Japan's Modified International Standards (JMIS) — inaugurated by the ASBJ in 2015 — are DEVELOPED through an "ENDORSEMENT" process applied to IFRS Standards issued by the IASB: JMIS ADOPTS IFRS Standards AS THEY STAND, EXCEPT for a SMALL, DELIBERATE number of specific "MODIFICATIONS" the ASBJ has determined WARRANT departure from the unmodified international text. There are, IN TOTAL, only TWO such modifications — THIS one (goodwill) and JMIS Modification No. 2 (Other Comprehensive Income — see the companion poster) — making JMIS, in PRACTICE, "IFRS with two narrow, well-defined carve-outs," RATHER than a broadly DIFFERENT framework.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Scope of This Modification</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Companies electing to apply JMIS for consolidated statements</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">This modification applies to LISTED companies ELECTING to prepare their CONSOLIDATED financial statements under JMIS (one of the FOUR permitted frameworks in Japan, alongside J-GAAP, "Designated IFRS" — unmodified IFRS as endorsed for use in Japan — and US GAAP), specifically GOVERNING the subsequent treatment of GOODWILL recognised in a business combination.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Modification Itself — Mandatory Goodwill Amortisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">A systematic charge, over a determined period</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">UNDER this modification, GOODWILL recognised in a business combination is AMORTISED SYSTEMATICALLY over its estimated USEFUL LIFE (subject to a MAXIMUM period, broadly CONSISTENT with the approach applied under J-GAAP's own ASBJ Statement No. 21 — see the companion poster) — REPLACING IFRS 3/IAS 36's UNMODIFIED international approach of treating goodwill as an INDEFINITE-life asset, tested ONLY for impairment, NEVER systematically amortised.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Why the ASBJ Insisted on This Departure From IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Concerns about impairment-only reliability and earnings management</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The ASBJ has LONG expressed CONCERN that an impairment-ONLY approach to goodwill can allow OVERSTATED goodwill balances to PERSIST on the Balance Sheet for EXTENDED periods, given the SUBJECTIVE, judgement-intensive nature of impairment TESTING (discount rate assumptions, cash flow projections) — creating a genuine RISK of DELAYED recognition of an economic DECLINE that has ALREADY occurred, and potential for EARNINGS management through the TIMING of impairment charges. SYSTEMATIC amortisation, by CONTRAST, provides a MORE MECHANICAL, OBJECTIVE, and PREDICTABLE reduction in goodwill's carrying amount over TIME — a PHILOSOPHY the ASBJ considered IMPORTANT enough to preserve EVEN within an otherwise IFRS-based framework.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Consistency With J-GAAP's Own Goodwill Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The same underlying philosophy across two different frameworks</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">This MODIFICATION ensures that GOODWILL is treated CONSISTENTLY whether a Japanese company reports under J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 21) OR elects JMIS — BOTH frameworks REQUIRE systematic amortisation, REFLECTING a SHARED underlying Japanese accounting PHILOSOPHY that transcends the CHOICE of overall reporting framework.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Impairment Testing Continues Alongside Amortisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A dual safeguard — systematic reduction, plus an impairment backstop</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">EVEN WITH mandatory amortisation, IMPAIRMENT testing CONTINUES to apply WHERE indicators of impairment EXIST — providing a DUAL safeguard: SYSTEMATIC, mechanical reduction over TIME, PLUS an additional impairment CHECK for SIGNIFICANT, UNANTICIPATED declines in value that AMORTISATION alone would not adequately CAPTURE.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Goodwill Under JMIS vs Unmodified IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The same acquisition, two very different subsequent treatments</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: ¥800,000,000 of goodwill, 10-year amortisation period</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company recognises ¥800,000,000 of goodwill on an acquisition, determining a 10-year amortisation period is appropriate.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Under <strong>JMIS</strong>, the company recognises ¥800,000,000 ÷ 10 = <strong>¥80,000,000 of amortisation EXPENSE per year</strong> — a MATERIAL, RECURRING P&amp;L charge. Under <strong>unmodified, unamended IFRS</strong> (or Designated IFRS as endorsed for Japan), the SAME ¥800,000,000 would SIMPLY remain on the Balance Sheet, tested ANNUALLY for impairment, with NO systematic amortisation charge AT ALL — a genuinely MATERIAL difference in REPORTED profitability between the TWO frameworks for the SAME underlying transaction.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — an Impairment Trigger Under JMIS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The dual safeguard in action</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A significant, unanticipated decline in the acquired business's performance</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, after 3 years of amortisation (carrying amount: ¥800,000,000 − 3×¥80,000,000 = ¥560,000,000), the acquired business unit's performance deteriorates significantly, triggering an impairment indicator.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the ONGOING systematic amortisation, the company STILL performs an IMPAIRMENT test — and, IF the recoverable amount is FOUND to be below ¥560,000,000, recognises an ADDITIONAL impairment LOSS for the shortfall — illustrating that AMORTISATION and IMPAIRMENT testing operate TOGETHER, not as ALTERNATIVES, under this modification.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What this modification requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Amortisation period and method applied to goodwill</span>
          <span>Amount of goodwill amortisation expense recognised for the period</span>
          <span>Impairment losses recognised, in addition to amortisation</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Determining an appropriate goodwill amortisation period</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Recoverable amount estimates when an impairment indicator arises alongside ongoing amortisation</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — JMIS (as modified) vs unmodified IFRS 3/IAS 36</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">JMIS (Modification No. 1)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">Unmodified IFRS (IFRS 3/IAS 36)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Goodwill amortisation</span><span>MANDATORY, systematic</span><span>NOT permitted — indefinite-life asset</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Impairment testing</span><span>Continues, alongside amortisation</span><span>The SOLE mechanism for reducing goodwill's carrying amount</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is JMIS's DEFINING, headline modification — the SINGLE clearest illustration of HOW Japan's endorsement process can PRESERVE a domestic accounting PREFERENCE even WITHIN an otherwise internationally-aligned framework.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every goodwill event lands under JMIS</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Goodwill amortisation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised systematically every period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Impairment loss (if triggered)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised in addition to ongoing amortisation</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 21</span><span>Business Combinations — supplies the same underlying goodwill amortisation philosophy this JMIS modification imports</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>JMIS Modification No. 2</span><span>The only other modification within the JMIS framework, addressing Other Comprehensive Income</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Japan's Modified International Standards (JMIS)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A framework adopting IFRS Standards as issued by the IASB, subject to exactly two ASBJ-determined modifications, one of which is this goodwill amortisation requirement.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
          <div class="glossary-term">Endorsement process</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">ASBJ's process of adopting IFRS Standards for JMIS purposes, incorporating them as issued except for the deliberate, narrow modifications.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP / JMIS terminology)</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet / Statement of Financial Position
        <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement
        <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部 (Equity)
        <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets / Equity
        <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="footer-note">JMIS Modification Accounting Standard No. 1 — Accounting for Goodwill · Extended Reference Poster · Self-contained, no internet connection required</div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
